{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to CuriosityStream for supporting PBS!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3200,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if there is no such thing as dark matter.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6400,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if our understanding \nof gravity is just wrong?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9920,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "New work is taking another \nshot at that Einstein guy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13520,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see if we’ve finally scored a hit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21040,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve now been searching for dark matter\nfor over half a century."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the early 60s, Vera Rubin proved that the\nspiral galaxies are rotating so fast that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30320,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they should fling themselves apart - assuming \nthey are held together by the gravity of their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34640,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "visible mass alone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They would need at least 5 times as much \nmatter to provide the gravity needed to hold"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40880,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these galaxies together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the gravity of visible matter is also\nway too weak to hold galaxy clusters together,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or to bend the path of light to the degree\nseen in gravitational lenses - when more distant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53840,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light sources are warped by an intervening\nmass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It sure looks like 80% of the mass in the\nuniverse is completely invisible to us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62960,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve dubbed this hypothetical stuff dark\nmatter, and of course we’ve talked about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter many times on this channel - from\nthe evidence for its existence to some of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the speculative ideas of what it might be\nmade of - from exotic particles to black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78000,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if we’ve been thinking about this\nthe wrong way all the time?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The expected rotation rates of galaxies come \nfrom applying our laws of gravity based on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87600,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the observed mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89200,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So … the mass could be wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91440,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or the laws of gravity could be wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, if your scientific theory doesn’t\nfit observations we should reject our theory,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99120,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99600,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And for nearly as long as astronomers have\nbeen hunting for dark matter, others have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been hunting for an alteration to our theory\nof gravity that can explain the effect of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108080,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter without the actual matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today, we’re going to look into that long\nhistory - what has worked and what has utterly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "failed - and finally at a new proposal that\npurports to fix those failures once and for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121600,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to Isaac Newton’s Law of Universal \nGravitation, the gravitational field drops"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127120,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "off with the square of distance from \nthe mass producing that gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In most galaxies, stars are somewhat concentrated \ntowards the centers, which means gravity should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136480,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weaken towards the outskirts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means the orbital velocities of stars\nout there should be lower in order to keep"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143920,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them in orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The so-called rotation curve should drop - orbital \nspeed should diminish with distance from center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark matter is supposed to add extra mass\nthat’s more evenly distributed through galaxies,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strengthening the gravitational field in the\noutskirts to explain the high rotation speeds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163600,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark matter flattens rotation curves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166320,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if gravity doesn’t obey Newton’s\nlaw of gravity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170320,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, we actually know that it doesn’t."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Albert Einstein found that Newtonian gravity \nbreaks down when the gravitational field gets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too strong - there you need his general theory \nof relativity, which explains gravity as the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "curvature in the fabric of space and time \nrather than just as a classical force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Einsteinian gravity looks exactly like\nNewtonian gravity when gravitational fields"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194160,
    "dDurationMs": 560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get weak."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195600,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if Einstein missed something?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What if Newtonian gravity breaks down \nboth for very strong AND very weak fields?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the idea behind Modified Newtonian\nDynamics, or MOND, proposed by Israeli physicist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211760,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mordehai Milgrom in 1982."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215120,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea is straightforward enough - what\nif there exists a minimum possible acceleration"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221360,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that can be produced by the gravitational\nforce?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224240,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In MOND, force or acceleration drop off with\ndistance squared until, at very low values"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230720,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they start to plateau out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This can be done with a modification to either \nNewton’s law of universal gravitation - in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which case gravity has a minimum strength\n- or by a modification to Newton’s 3rd law"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of motion, in which case the acceleration\nproduced by a force has a minimum strength."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248640,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you tune the modification right you recover \nthe observed rotation curves for spiral galaxies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254640,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very nicely without the need for extra mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257920,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you only need to tune a single parameter - \nwhich is effectively the minimum acceleration"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262720,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- to get the correct rotation curves for nearly\nall galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s very promising, but in order to be\ntaken seriously, a new hypothesis like MOND"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needs to do a few things. One:\nit needs to give the right answer in more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279840,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than one special case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So MOND would need to do away with the need \nfor physical dark matter in the other places"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287280,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we see evidence for dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290080,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two: it needs to be consistent with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293280,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the other known laws and theories \nof physics that are experimentally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297200,
    "dDurationMs": 640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "verified."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And three: it needs to make testable predictions \nbeyond the phenomena that it was tuned for."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305280,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s take these one by one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307200,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, how does MOND do with respect to the\nother evidence for dark matter?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312320,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not … great actually."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you tune MOND to work for galaxies and\nthen apply it to galaxy clusters, you do get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319680,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rid of the need for some of the dark matter\nbut not all of it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You still need about 20% of the current dark\nmatter requirement to explain all the gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328400,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we see in clusters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now you might think that cutting down the\ninvisible mass requirement by 80% is pretty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335040,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good - and it is helpful to be honest."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the fact that you still need some type\nof physical dark matter in clusters is seen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a strong point against MOND in its first\nincarnation at least."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are some other pieces of evidence for\ndark matter that O-G MOND also fails for,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354240,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but I’ll come back to those."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356480,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now Point 2."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358720,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Is MOND consistent with the rest of physics?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363120,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No. It’s totally broken."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It doesn’t respect conservation of energy\nor momentum or angular momentum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it’s not consistent with general relativity \n- in that general relativity does not reproduce"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375280,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MOND in what we call the “weak field limit.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead it does what it was designed to do\n- it reproduces good ol’ Newtonian gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383280,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not looking good for MOND."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385280,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let’s address point 3 anyway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387840,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Does MOND make any predictions beyond \nthe observations that inspired it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392080,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is actually where we can turn this around."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395840,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Spiral galaxies all follow this tight relationship \nbetween their speed of rotation and their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400640,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "luminosity - the brighter they are the faster\nthey spin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404800,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the Tully-Fisher Law."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a little surprising that the Tully-Fisher Law \nis such a tight relationship because the rotation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "velocity depends on the dark matter halo \nwhile the luminosity depends on the stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now those two are connected, but some believe \nthat their connection shouldn’t be so perfect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422800,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to give the extremely tight Tully-Fisher law."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, if you tune MOND to get\nthe flat rotation curves of spiral galaxies,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you automatically get the correct relationship \nbetween rotation speed and luminosity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435920,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was a completely unexpected \nand un-engineered outcome of MOND."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, while the Tully-Fisher Law was already \nknown, we can sort of count it as a prediction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446400,
    "dDurationMs": 640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of MOND."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this one success has been enough to inspire \nothers to dig deeper into the idea over the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453120,
    "dDurationMs": 560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The next critical step was to get a version\nof MOND that didn’t contradict so much of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458800,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the rest of physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459840,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For that Jacob Bekenstein came to the rescue."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You may remember Bekenstein from such hit \nideas as the Bekenstein bound, which connects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468640,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole information content to entropy,\nas well as other black-hole-related awesomeness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1984 he diverted his attention for a moment \nto work with Mordehai Milgrom in fixing MOND."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first step was to reformulate \nMOND using Lagrangian mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488240,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What on earth does that mean, you ask?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately we just did an episode on the\nawesome power of the Lagrangian."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There we saw that the principle of least action \nallows equations of motion to be extracted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501520,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a way that automatically obeys all of our\nconservation laws."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505760,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And done the right way the result can also\nwork with relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bekenstein and Milgrom achieved this \nby adding a second field to gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515680,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Einstein’s description, the gravitational "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517600,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field is what we call a tensor \nfield - a multi-component"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520960,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object that describes the curvature of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These guys added a new scalar field - a field \nthat’s just a single numerical value everywhere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529600,
    "dDurationMs": 560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it was a good start - the resulting \n“AQuaL - for “a quadratic Lagrangian” gave the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same results as MOND, except that \nconservation laws were obeyed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541680,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and because this was a relativistic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543840,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory it was possible to see if it gave the\nright result for the bending of light by galaxies,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549440,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which wasn’t even possible with the original\nMOND."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552640,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it did not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AQuaL also had the unfortunate prediction\nof faster-than-light waves in this added scalar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561360,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field, which broke causality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not to be deterred, Bekenstein came \nback over 20 years later with an update."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569120,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If adding one field doesn’t work, why not\nadd another?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572720,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2005 Bekenstein introduced TeVeS, for Tensor \nVector Scalar gravity - based on the fact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it describes gravity with three fields\n- a tensor, a vector, and a scalar."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585280,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The introduction of the new field fixed the\nproblem with gravitational lensing and also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589760,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tamed the awkward \ncausality-breaking nature of AQuaL."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It acted like Newtonian mechanics on solar\nsystem scales, like MOND on galactic scales,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598320,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and like regular general relativity \nfor gravitational lensing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601760,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was not without problems though - for example \nthe physicist Michael Seifert claimed that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "TeVeS and other MOND proposals produce \ninstabilities in the presence of matter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613040,
    "dDurationMs": 640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which would, for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613680,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example, make long-lived stars impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617280,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the main problem with TeVeS \nis cosmological in nature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the most important pieces of evidence \nfor dark matter as a particle is seen in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626640,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light that comes from the very early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The cosmic microwave background radiation \nreveals a lumpiness that tells us how matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635840,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pulled itself together under its \nown gravity at the earliest times."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640400,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Back then, light and matter were locked \ntogether due to the extreme densities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Regular matter was kept from collapsing into \nany structures by the pressure of the intense"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649680,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation of that era."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But dark matter doesn’t interact with light,\nso it would have been able to collapse just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657120,
    "dDurationMs": 320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fine."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And after the universe had expanded and cooled \nenough for regular matter to be released from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the clutch of light, it could have followed\nthe dark matter into its deep gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668320,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wells and get to the business of forming galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if dark matter isn’t real, and regular\nmatter controls gravity completely, then no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677600,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structure should have been able to form at\nthose early times."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For this reason, most forms of MOND \n- including TeVeS, come up short."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686240,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is where the new guys come in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688320,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2020 Constantinos Skordis and Tom Złosnik \nproposed a new relativistic version of MOND,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695600,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and just last month their paper passed peer\nreview."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their big change was that they allowed the\nscalar field to change its behavior over time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They managed to tweak their equations so that \nin the early universe, that field behaved"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a bit like a type of matter, which Złosnik\ncalls “dark dust”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was able to clump in the right \nway to kickstart cluster formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then later its behavior shifted so that \nit now behaves more like Bekenstein’s TeVeS"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727040,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proposal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More work is needed to see if the newly-dubbled \nRelMOND - relativistic MOND - works for galaxy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734320,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clusters and keeps stars from exploding \n- but the authors are optimistic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739200,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so, problem solved."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741040,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t need dark matter, anymore?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743680,
    "dDurationMs": 800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not so fast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Modified gravity theories still can’t explain\nthe Bullet Cluster - and I don’t have time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750960,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get into that and we’ve covered it before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So I’ll just say that when galaxy clusters\ncollide and the dark matter gets ripped away"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the light matter - it makes you doubt\nthat dark matter is just light matter acting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764320,
    "dDurationMs": 400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "funny."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765440,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course there are MOND proposals which claim \nto address this, but the Bullet Cluster might"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770000,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be the most awkward result \nfor modified gravity folks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774480,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this point the two theories are in a bloody\ntheoretical knife fight, where the knife is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780320,
    "dDurationMs": 880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Occam’s razor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Proponents of dark-matter-as-particle say\nthat MOND proposals are now so elaborate and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787760,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fine-tuned that we can’t take them seriously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But MOND proponents say that it’s the behavior of \ndark matter particles that have to be carefully"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fine-tuned to produce the phenomena that MOND \npredicts naturally - like the flatness of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803120,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rotation curves and the Tully-Fisher law."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806160,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Who’s right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well the majority of experts are pretty firmly\nin the dark-matter-as-particle camp."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812960,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although our experiments haven’t \ndetected dark matter yet, there are still"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plenty of possibilities for what it might\nbe beyond our standard model of particle physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821600,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we’ve been through those before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Bekenstein was no slouch, nor are many of \nthe others who have supported MOND theories."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829280,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can’t dismiss them out of hand."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I personally withhold my judgement - because \nit’s OK to be uncertain, and because it’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838320,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be equally exciting whichever way this gets\nresolved."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One way or another we opened paths to continue \nour exploration of reality, whether we’re"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "led beyond the standard model by dark matter \nparticles, or beyond general relativity by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852000,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hidden gravitational modes of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855040,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A big thank you to CuriosityStream for supporting\nPBS!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857920,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CuriosityStream is SmartTV for your SmartTV."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861360,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The subscription streaming service offers\ndocumentaries and non¬fiction titles from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864880,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "various filmmakers, with topics including\nHistory, Nature, Science, Food, Technology,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869840,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Travel, and more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For instance, CuriosityStream has Black Holes:\nMessages from The Edge of Space, which examines"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876960,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not only black holes, but neutrino astronomy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It takes a deep dive into the science of black \nholes and takes you into the Antarctic lab"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884880,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where astrophysicists detected neutrinos in\nthe ice of the South Pole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890080,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are also collections of curated programs\nselected by experts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For more information, go to \ncuriositystream.com/PBSSPACETIME "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899120,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and use the code SPACETIME for a trial."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get to comments, we want to tell\nyou about PBS’s new medical show called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905680,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Vitals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906720,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s always been important to stay healthy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s gotten harder to tell what medical\ninformation is based in science and what is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913600,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unhelpful pseudoscience."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately, Vitals, PBS’s brand new health\nand wellness show, is here to help."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Co-hosts Dr. Alok Patel and nurse Sheena Williams \nwill bust medical myths, explore the latest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928160,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "science and answer all your burning health\nquestions in every episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932000,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Check out Vitals in the link in the description,\nand tell them that Space Time sent you!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our last episode was all about the principle \nof least action, and how this one simple idea"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941680,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sort of leads to all of physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944560,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see what you had to say."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945720,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "J Smith asks, if the configuration  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949200,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space Lagrangian seems to bridge \nquantum mechanics and relativity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952720,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what's missing to make this \na theory of everything?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956640,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rather than answer this myself, I willl read\nthe reply by Fernando, the co-writer of that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961280,
    "dDurationMs": 560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In simple terms, the universe at its very\ncore seems to be a set of symmetries which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967760,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are manifest in the Lagrangian."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970400,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This means that if we knew all the symmetries \nthe universe follows we could describe it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perfectly, but we don't know all the symmetries \nand we are not sure how those symmetries fit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980480,
    "dDurationMs": 640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981680,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well put, Fernando."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the symmetries of the Lagrangian via\nNoether’s theorem that yields our conservation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988960,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "laws and ultimately, well, all of physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Check our episodes on Noether’s theorem,\nquantum invariance, and the electroweak force"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996720,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for some details, but we probably need to\ngo even deeper."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000320,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jackie Johnson asks - in the case of gravitational \nlensing, isn't the light still traveling in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005120,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a straight line?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006320,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Isn’t it spacetime that bends, not light?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010000,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s a valid way to think of it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Light does travel a straight line if you look\nat an infinitesimally small patch of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine light traveling through curved space\nas like an and walking across a disco ball."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024800,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ant’s path around a disco ball looks \ncurved, even if it travels in perfectly straight"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029280,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lines across each mirror."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, in space the disco ball mirrors are\ninfinitesimally small, but over those regions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035920,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the path is straight."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A few people pointed out an error - I said\nthat the action reduces to an integral over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043440,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proper time in general relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046000,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was right - but I then went on to call\nthis a “principle of least proper time”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050480,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by analogy to the principle of least action."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, in general relativity objects in\ngravitational fields tend to maximize, not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1057760,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minimize their proper time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1059920,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s still consistent with the whole action\nthing because the proper name is the principle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of stationary action - and the maximum is\nalso a stationary point - of proper time and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070720,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the action."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071760,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I was still misleading."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073360,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks for correcting me on that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many of you point out that you’re already\nadherents of the principle of least action."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1080400,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As in you take the fastest, easiest, or laziest\npath to any outcome."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084720,
    "dDurationMs": 400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Me too!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085680,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like, for example, when I come up with a joke\nto end the comment section  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107280,
    "dDurationMs": 80,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "…"
    } ]
  } ]
}
